#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/2e/2ee58da2733a468406869e43f57deebc6f7924f1d1149c0ca9391ea9995d9f87.png)\nwhere ![](images/83/83596c7f3038e585eb35ffafc966810e6891b55fc4d1efb04494bdc4b3755b05.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/af/af5b69c599a13a707365e88f6f50939064c7b199a1d8231b32a1c88e425440fa.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/83/833ce06c0c6d5f9beea87a84ef1b286748472c312987e837ca6f47f60225b1fa.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/fb/fb015d0935718dd6ffdfb9eb13b2b8fd0c255c63a2f5cecdb988a3f83df14685.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/03/03f2711cb5a80e1e94f879bf8a68c25db27f8280f5e1e344425f9ee2a334a829.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/bd/bd4d36cd68896e6360317a0c01c92841423020218436d85ae07eb413463fffa8.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/91/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597" :question/problem "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/34/343f6f32ac21a1bf06b86174734dd9ebba6ddb7598cbe83b4ade59f70e8e8b26.png)\nwhere ![](images/cb/cb0f32e55af289452f1429ddfe3214a46501c55f3a61deaf10b4f736e47dca7f.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/85/85fdb4fc83a6f2b44178be2e97a0e7985d97350deb4e0fb123e513a80090906a.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/3f/3f984a493e976bd522455395defcb4c1562d61ca56e19fa27e2272832ce6011c.png)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/44/44f82894493c95c38039bc1e6a842bb7226571e9818e3c10c50d33627464ea37.png)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/7f/7f53fdda091242e4fee1edd70a7e96c649eff8bac93fcdc9fed6da38ec5c7523.png)\nwhere ![](images/3b/3b9909cba711a2394b8dbcda4b9d72ffbd9d37bd24b90506ac22b44c4cd398c4.png)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](images/1e/1ea46da3e9697ee89d1112dfec53457657271c391a28a6c55267da70d38157c4.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/97/9725d696549426d16093b7cd0e9bb188589472ed9ff81520c19a07ab296fb96d.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](images/85/85e8d745e2b504770815bb92ad1a151f422c941874f33840593836e78eeaacde.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/2f/2f67b02cda725daa0d0b994f3f4952065793e53ddc32260b14310f5158876f4f.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2b/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/70/703fb7adc0ca076e42aecc781d4d0dc58eb5f4fec03db4d18bf5a36f7055a349.png)\nwhere ![](images/72/7210186106fc202e3b9a35ffa32b06b09493ce28c191a89c2824a35a547febff.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/f2/f292220b15ff9b0d695cb8b9f2349cc275557fd2b77c32c147e25365ab0f7e47.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/56/5671487738e750f488ad61852f17fb4b6b14cf4050fee66338000d4939914ce6.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/65/651cc55c8b96c402fc00140c06736493e71276666ce4eb1ba74c2294600a1b89.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/22/222dcd944fc89cd0f07970cddf5c50aa167c7f2d25b1f7305779c3dbb0a4a6af.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/08/08ad54b92f62513fb88f12f7bd6da358263e46673d686e0982a1e8044b8ab131.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/c5/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/6c/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2453f6e4-454d-56ac-bc8e-8f90b1cabe02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302687} {:answer/id #uuid "25c968e3-c729-5639-a3ca-cee68536dead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302699} {:answer/id #uuid "7394b45d-420f-52bf-bbc5-96da7f80405a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303156} {:answer/id #uuid "b9434a51-00db-5650-a51f-ba47f7ee2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2453f6e4-454d-56ac-bc8e-8f90b1cabe02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302687} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d2b03a-cb1a-519b-9c31-766de086e40a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a254cbd4-64a4-5677-b4de-e68a9fdd633f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303456} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffdda8-340a-5a7f-8516-875ce2fdcb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303498} {:answer/id #uuid "f45f4210-507a-5d8f-9dae-805885e0b36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a254cbd4-64a4-5677-b4de-e68a9fdd633f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303456} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18eae7b-a5bc-50c8-a851-a35904c65972" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4041ca-7f05-5692-9e7a-a49f5d0072f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302556} {:answer/id #uuid "798e32cc-ea75-5415-a467-de9e3f5173e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303207} {:answer/id #uuid "e58ab17d-33c7-558f-9231-284eeefb8c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798e32cc-ea75-5415-a467-de9e3f5173e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303207} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcbdc676-89c3-590c-9ac8-13ae5deba4e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6644c70-dc61-58f2-81c3-a23eae94c4cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-331517" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a2b290-8039-5ca4-94f1-b39d0c36e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302486} {:answer/id #uuid "3c581c58-1d13-52d9-9e42-5b001ed96302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302831} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd8149-e3f4-5a37-93f4-a3a274e3a24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/8715fffa8fec9468edef2194b75842cb79de3bc9a7db6c0d8d23f44c6eb5368b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302991} {:answer/id #uuid "bab62c71-181b-5361-b434-80130ab31878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d70a32516a4c051a8f4dc7460a313afe817fde14896cb108d5978858b58864ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303609} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0ba666-971f-56d1-93d5-ff65be164c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a2b290-8039-5ca4-94f1-b39d0c36e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302486} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ccddb7d-5291-5236-a231-6e5f34e99835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f707130-0164-506c-911a-bb075d503cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332271" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/f5/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101fadb7-e4a4-5775-a692-421ec4666605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9eb64f436febd0b3cbc108920317ca8eed58139f453347033f1bdedd74f08f42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302562} {:answer/id #uuid "18572fb1-6da2-5ca5-a7d8-8e72f0a57a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/374e12736ee4cf016f75711f67b7d6d1696f48cc6288f13df236812998c4202c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302618} {:answer/id #uuid "23281918-757d-59a9-936e-99e2d29adc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a52abbca3f2186e1a5d6ca6c33646718484312e3aa1cc066e3c0f8a1b5f1152a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302679} {:answer/id #uuid "76e633ec-8b8c-54e1-a839-d9e107941080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd15cec76717d417e1ee906c10abaddc77c7095d4e20c95a90afed21a1592c01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303189} {:answer/id #uuid "86f7dd7e-15f1-5819-8a6c-7d22f4e5e029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f7dd7e-15f1-5819-8a6c-7d22f4e5e029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303301} :answer-field/id #uuid "6acb4b83-1689-5f64-be06-1db39bd674d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7525f92-11a2-54b3-8c7c-6d2f543e65d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332579" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/37/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21101184-6e57-5bc3-8ab7-9d820de38ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a5d09c1f7b7201f8910e7fe029bb9b1759e266bea84e04eb14079deb803a0255.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302664} {:answer/id #uuid "23c092c2-5693-59f1-9f2e-fe5f382649e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6917abe8fa178aa3ffb889255e58faa6cda94947fce44e42516637552c19fed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302682} {:answer/id #uuid "57eeb44b-e7a2-5c28-b847-7633f0f0b49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a8ce97b61b596096070ed52d063c80294d2353be6776cb5658e060d33997c6aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302992} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e607c8-f17f-5d7c-8bb0-b75b5142ece8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303671} {:answer/id #uuid "ec50c2d6-40fb-57f1-97d4-cfd7f3fa59c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6623c5f1563832e5999daafef510551eecc4fd17419f90fa651283fc8de30f1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e607c8-f17f-5d7c-8bb0-b75b5142ece8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303671} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fba251d-4700-5eba-ab1d-cbd0d507bf2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14b93aa5-65d3-5ddc-9724-ab937ea4f1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332678" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c7bc7d-0232-5fe1-a6d4-6f9a22440550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c483bbf926df156b7f85fbedcbb6ab18571ff4f544416cd8ea554b70b59a589.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391045} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4028ce-9f71-5bd4-ad6b-47272c4ba666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27c6bf8d11d3de50beac351f88104df58cac31053eb6ecfc3a35ce77ccd8600b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391046} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f6682-16a0-5a4e-b948-d2adbb5ee402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01d93cb2e68123b061b057ce30b94bcce44401b2d0fc0c566b3c33478c07fb92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391047} {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391048} {:answer/id #uuid "e571bbe0-b813-51a0-a84b-7bcbc9e1fb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/389d3cfc92ece3a63300767f92c34c11dccec981ac43f3f66a2ced02906f8a73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391048} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f7e5e5-ba46-5c75-bde7-3c2b00f02f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332683" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4637eefd-6ffa-5302-a1bf-464d7d3655e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302900} {:answer/id #uuid "bd36e0e8-924e-5434-885b-28aa9e03a597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303617} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e1bd02-e825-5c61-8342-967c4baa3a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303650} {:answer/id #uuid "c42e116e-7a9f-57e0-871d-2274f8465da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303662} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d93aa3-5f2a-5d35-85b1-a93ba6faafae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d93aa3-5f2a-5d35-85b1-a93ba6faafae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303743} :answer-field/id #uuid "39400a04-b082-53e0-a216-5351b940bb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f20c0d44-466d-592f-adfd-5535d1164b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-332705" :question/problem "![](images/98/98ffa8fec669ce09d22b5b904b710632314f7b22e0eb5098719c41147c186975.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c5b7a3-26ff-50f5-94e9-6d8844fb678c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302575} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3d43fe-787c-5177-bbac-12bebe043929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302751} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0d4ee-e24f-5d9c-b2b7-0d001e462222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302835} {:answer/id #uuid "92871808-ead1-5f60-af6a-dad167814456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303367} {:answer/id #uuid "f06a5725-94e7-58bc-b5b3-9291ccbbab16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92871808-ead1-5f60-af6a-dad167814456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303367} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c07c39-3